<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE topic SYSTEM "https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/xhtml-entities.dtd">
<topic xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
       xsi:noNamespaceSchemaLocation="https://resources.jetbrains.com/writerside/1.0/topic.v2.xsd"
       id="reporting" title="Report rather than throw">

    <show-structure for="chapter" depth="2"/>

    <tldr>
        <p>
            Requires a weaver your own code builds —
            <a href="what-a-plugin-can-do.topic">What a plugin can do</a>.
        </p>
    </tldr>

    <p>
        A diagnostic you report carries a code and a message, and names the weave that caused
        it; a remedy costs one more call. A throw carries none of that. The engine catches a
        throw at five named phases; each guard decides what it costs. Those five phases are not
        a perimeter around plugin code.
    </p>

    <code-block lang="mermaid">
flowchart TB
    A["Instantiation guard"] -->|throws| A2["Your plugin never loads"]
    A --> S["Sorted again, no guard"]
    S -->|throws| X["No diagnostic at all"]
    S --> B["Contribute guard"]
    B -->|throws| B2["Every registration is discarded"]
    B --> C["Planning guard, one PointSpec"]
    C -->|throws| C2["Every site that spec would find"]
    C --> D["Apply guard, whole class rewrite"]
    D -->|throws| D2["Original bytes handed back"]
    D --> E["Observe guard"]
    E -->|throws| E2["Warning only, and weaving goes on"]
    </code-block>

    <chapter title="What you report with" id="handles">
        <p>
            Every reporting handle the SPI gives you is a <code>Reporter</code>. All of them end
            at the one <code>DiagnosticListener</code> the driver installed.
            <a href="spi.topic#report">The plugin SPI</a> lists them all.
        </p>

        <deflist type="full">
            <def title="A code of your own">
                <p>
                    Build one from <code>PluginDiagnosticId</code>, never from a framework code. It
                    carries your namespace, so the message traces back to you.
                    <a href="namespaces.topic">Namespaces and identifiers</a> has the spelling.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="A remedy, which costs one more call">
                <p>
                    <code>report(code, message)</code> gives the code's default severity, an
                    unknown location, and no remedy. <code>Diagnostic.builder(...)</code> adds the
                    location, the detail and the remedy. The remedy is the only part that says
                    what to write instead.
                </p>
            </def>
            <def title="Reporting stops nothing by itself">
                <p>
                    <code>report</code> returns void. An error you report neither fails the build
                    nor withdraws a declaration. That is the driver's call, taken from the severity
                    afterwards — <a href="diagnosing-a-weave.topic">Diagnosing a weave</a>. Stop
                    yourself: answer with no sites, or with
                    <code>Injector.Emitter.NOTHING</code>.
                </p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <note>
            <p>
                The reporter handed to <code>Injector.validate</code> is the one call site whose
                severities change what the engine does. An error there abandons the declaration
                before any bytes are emitted. <code>WeaverBuilder</code>'s explain-report footer
                also reads severities, but only to count them.
            </p>
        </note>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What a throw costs" id="cost">
        <p>
            Five guards, five blast radii. Each catches <code>Throwable</code> and turns it into
            the phase's diagnostic. Only <code>VirtualMachineError</code> is re-thrown untouched.
            <a href="loading-plugins.topic">Loading and isolating plugins</a> covers the boundary
            itself.
        </p>

        <table style="header-row">
            <tr><td>Throwing while</td><td>What it costs</td><td>Instead</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Being constructed</td><td><code>AW3114</code>, that plugin</td>
                <td>Return a constant from both</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Contributing</td><td><code>AW3115</code>, every registration</td>
                <td>Report through <code>diagnostics()</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Resolving a point</td><td><code>AW3116</code>, every matched site</td>
                <td>Report, then answer with no sites</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Emitting bytes</td><td><code>AW3117</code>, the entire class</td>
                <td>Report, then answer <code>NOTHING</code></td></tr>
            <tr><td>Observing an event</td><td><code>AW3118</code>, a warning</td>
                <td>Repair the observer; nothing broke</td></tr>
        </table>

        <p>
            The first row also covers <code>id()</code> and <code>apiLevel()</code>: a malformed
            service declaration costs every plugin on that loader, still as <code>AW3114</code>.
            An emitter that throws <code>IllegalArgumentException</code> costs its class as
            <code>AW4003</code> or <code>AW4004</code> instead of <code>AW3117</code> — the class
            writer catches that one first, ahead of this guard. A declaration whose
            <code>require</code> is left at its default of 1 is also refused as
            <code>AW1043</code> when its point throws. The resolution that would have satisfied
            it never ran.
        </p>

        <p>
            The fourth row is the one to design around. That guard wraps the rewrite of the whole
            class, entered whenever any contributed kind takes part. It costs every declaration
            in that class, built-in ones included. The message names the contributed kind
            identifiers taking part, comma-joined and possibly from more than one plugin, not any
            one plugin by itself. <a href="pipeline.topic">The weaving pipeline</a> has what a
            rewrite covers, and <a href="diagnostics-policy.topic">the AW3xxx codes</a> the
            severities.
        </p>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="Where the guard does not reach" id="gaps">
        <p>
            Calls that run outside the five phases above are not wrapped by any of them. A throw
            from one of these reaches <code>Weaver.weave</code> or <code>WeaverBuilder.build()</code>
            with no diagnostic at all.
        </p>

        <deflist type="narrow">
            <def title="The candidate sort's id()">
                <p>A second call, after instantiation and before contribution, once per build.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="A contributed point on a slice bound">
                <p>The planning guard reads the declaration's own <code>@At</code>, so a built-in
                    one leaves the bound unguarded. Answer with no sites instead of throwing: the
                    slice reports <code>AW1120</code> or <code>AW1121</code> for you.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="Looking up an injector by kind">
                <p>Before every apply guard, once per declaration.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="validate and stackOperandsAt">
                <p>Before every apply guard, once per declaration.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="A factory's kinds(), ids(), aliases()">
                <p>After the contribute guard has already returned.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="plugin.id() in PluginRegistry.plugins()">
                <p>Once per stamped class, while the weave record is written.</p>
            </def>
            <def title="plugin.id().describe()">
                <p>An argument to the observe guard, evaluated before the guard is entered.</p>
            </def>
        </deflist>

        <p>
            Return a constant everywhere a factory or an injector is asked for its identity. Keep
            whatever backs it out of a static initialiser. Two calls the guards do reach still
            cost more than their phase suggests:
        </p>

        <list>
            <li><p><code>PluginContext.metadata</code> throws past its bounds rather than
                reporting. The throw lands inside the contribute guard, so exceeding a bound
                still costs the whole plugin, as <code>AW3115</code>. Stay inside the limits
                <a href="spi.topic#throws">The plugin SPI</a> gives.</p></li>
            <li><p>A <code>WeavePolicy</code> is not guarded at all. Refuse a class with a
                <code>Decision.Deny</code>, which carries a code and a reason. A throw there
                costs the run rather than the class.
                <a href="policy-and-safety.topic">Policy and safety</a> has what each driver does
                with it.</p></li>
        </list>
    </chapter>

    <chapter title="What you can hear back" id="observe">
        <p>
            Nothing. <code>PluginContext.diagnosticListeners(...)</code> is accepted, and the
            registry keeps what you pass. No part of the engine asks the registry for it, so a
            plugin is never handed a diagnostic.
        </p>

        <p>
            The four events are all that reaches you, and none carries a diagnostic.
            <code>WeavingFinished</code> brings counters, and the failures it counts are
            verification refusals rather than reports.
            <a href="plugin-lifecycle.topic">The plugin lifecycle</a> has the four in order. Keep
            your own record as you report, on the thread you reported from.
        </p>
    </chapter>

